---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import GifAudioToMp3 from '@/components/tools/media/GifAudioToMp3.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="amr-to-mp3">
  <GifAudioToMp3
    client:load
    accept=".amr,.3ga,.3gp,.awb,audio/amr,audio/3gpp"
    sourceLabel="AMR"
    sourceHint="Call recordings, voicemail and old phone memos: .amr, .3ga and .3gp all work"
    target="mp3"
    defaultBitrate="96"
    formatNote="AMR is a narrowband speech codec: everything it recorded fits comfortably into a 64–96 kbps MP3. A higher setting adds bytes, not clarity."
  />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>What AMR is, and why nothing plays it</h2>
    <p>
      Adaptive Multi-Rate is the speech codec that GSM networks used for voice calls. It encodes a
      narrowband signal (about 300 Hz to 3.4 kHz, the telephone band) at eight rates between 4.75 and
      12.2 kbps, and changes rate as network conditions change. It is very efficient for speech and
      unsuitable for anything else.
    </p>
    <p>
      AMR came from mobile telephony, and most desktop software ignored it. Windows and macOS do not
      open a bare <code>.amr</code> without extra codecs, most editors refuse it, and transcription
      services often reject it on upload. Android call recorders, Samsung and Nokia voice memo apps,
      dashcams and many voicemail systems still produce it.
    </p>

    <h2>The four extensions you will encounter</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>.amr is a bare AMR stream with a six-byte magic header. This is the usual case.</li>
      <li>.3ga is the Samsung name for AMR (or sometimes AAC) in a 3GP container. The audio is the same; the wrapper is different.</li>
      <li>.3gp is the general 3GPP container, which can also hold video. Only the audio track is converted.</li>
      <li>.awb is AMR-Wideband, a later 16 kHz variant used for HD Voice. It is clearer, and this tool handles it the same way.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Converting will not make it sound better</h2>
    <p>
      AMR discarded all audio outside the telephone band at the time of recording. Those frequencies
      are not in the file. A 320 kbps encode gives a large MP3 with the same narrowband audio: only
      the wrapper changes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Conversion gives you a file that opens everywhere and that you can edit, send to a
      transcription service or join to other recordings. It does not improve quality. A product that
      advertises AMR enhancement sells a noise gate.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use 64–96 kbps mono. This is more than the source contains, and it keeps a one-hour call
      recording under 45 MB.
    </p>

    <h2>Getting the most out of a poor recording</h2>
    <p>
      If the recording is hard to hear, these steps help more than the format:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Force mono. The source has one channel, and stereo only duplicates it at twice the size.</li>
      <li>Normalise the loudness with the option in the advanced panel. AMR recordings are often very quiet, and at −16 LUFS they are easier to hear and to transcribe.</li>
      <li>Do the real cleanup in an audio editor. A high-pass filter at 200 Hz and gentle compression help more than any converter setting. Convert to <a href="/mp3-to-wav">WAV</a> first if the editor prefers it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why these files in particular should never be uploaded</h2>
    <p>
      AMR files are call recordings, voicemail, dictated notes, interviews and evidence for disputes.
      This is some of the most sensitive audio people own. An online converter sends it to an unknown
      server under a policy you cannot audit.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing is uploaded here. The AMR decoder is part of a WebAssembly build of FFmpeg served from
      this site, and it runs in your browser tab. The Content-Security-Policy of the site permits no
      third-party origin. To verify, open the Network tab or turn off Wi-Fi, then convert.
    </p>

    <h2>If a file will not decode</h2>
    <p>
      There are two usual causes. The file is truncated (call recorders often stop mid-write when a
      call ends or the phone runs out of storage), or it is not AMR, because a file manager renamed it
      during a transfer. A valid AMR file starts with the ASCII bytes <code>#!AMR</code>; a 3GP file
      has <code>ftyp3gp</code> near the start. For other files, use the
      <a href="/audio-converter">general audio converter</a>, which detects the container itself.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
